  Abstract                 = {Eighteen papers present research in the field of political economy, focusing on the integration of economics and politics and the way institutions affect social decisions. Papers discuss transaction cost politics in the map of the New Institutionalism; political transitions in ancient Greece and medieval Italy--an analytic narrative; a collective-action theory of fiscal-military state building; stable constitutions in political transition; quandaries of gridlock and leadership in U.S. electoral politics; subcentral governments and debt crisis in Spain over the period 2000-2011; deciding how to choose the health care system; challenges to the standard Euclidean spatial model; a nonexistence theorem for clientelism in spatial models; nonseparable preferences and issue packaging in elections; when will incumbents avoid a primary challenge?--aggregation of partial information about candidates' valence; measuring the latent quality of precedent--scoring vertices in a network; the politics of austerity--modeling British attitudes toward public spending cuts; modeling elections with varying party bundles--applications to the 2004 Canadian election; a spatial model of elections in Turkey--tracing changes in the party system in the 2000s; do competitive districts necessarily produce centrist politicians?; a heteroskedastic spatial model of the vote--a model with application to the United States; and inferring ideological ambiguity from survey data. Schofield is William R. Taussig Professor of Political Economy and Professor in the Departments of Economics and Political Science at Washington University in St. Louis. Caballero is Associate Professor of Economics with the Faculty of Economics at the University of Vigo. Kselman is Research Fellow with the Center for Advanced Studies in the Social Sciences at the Juan March Institute. No index.},
